answered from the store: identical content')\n    eq(B, T2.rows.where.value.cwd, 'the stored cwd is THIS tree\\'s')\n    eq(B .. '/x.h', T2.rows.where.value.file)\n    eq({ '-I' .. B .. '/inc', '-I' .. B .. '0' }, T2.rows.where.value.flags, 'a path inside a flag follows; a SIBLING path is not this tree')\n    eq('#define X 1\\n', T2.rows.text.value)\nend)\n\ntest('facts cache: a warm derive answers every call from the store, and each INPUT re-derives exactly what it reaches — tree, scope, code, a loaded sibling, a need', function ()","file":"tests/cinterp_facts_spec.lua","before":"test('facts cache: a warm derive answers every call from the store, and each INPUT re-derives exactly what it reaches — tree, scope, code, a loaded sibling, a need', function ()"},"touched":["tests/cinterp_facts_spec.lua"],"verb":"edit"},"verb":"edit","bytes":1462,"decided_by":"tactic"},{"id":"1791217892.295233-edit","decisions":[],"invocation":{"args":{"after":"    -- (a path INSIDE a flag follows the tree; a SIBLING path `<A>0` is not inside A, so it is left exactly as derived)\n    local A = vim.fn.fnamemodify(a, ':p'):gsub('/$', '')\n    eq({ '-I' .. B .. '/inc', '-I' .. A .. '0' }, T2.rows.where.value.flags)","file":"tests/cinterp_facts_spec.lua","before":"    eq({ '-I' .. B .. '/inc', '-I' .. B .. '0' }, T2.rows.where.value.flags, 'a path inside a flag follows; a SIBLING path is not this tree')"},"touched":["tests/cinterp_facts_spec.lua"],"verb":"edit"},"verb":"edit","bytes":254,"decided_by":"tactic"},{"id":"1791218325.751896-edit","decisions":[],"invocation":{"args":{"after":"test('bytes: ★ the static degree MATCHES the measured allocation growth, function by function', function ()\n    if not has_lua() then skip 'no lua parser' end\n    -- (COVER turns the JIT off and runs a line hook: the measured allocation is the instrument's, not the function's)\n    if vim.env.COVER and vim.env.COVER ~= '' then skip 'COVER: a line hook and JIT-off change what an allocation measurement sees' end","file":"tests/loopcost_spec.lua","before":"test('bytes: ★ the static degree MATCHES the measured allocation growth, function by function', function ()\n    if not has_lua() then skip 'no lua parser' end"},"touched":["tests/loopcost_spec.lua"],"verb":"edit"},"verb":"edit","bytes":254,"decided_by":"tactic"},{"id":"1791218351.005342-edit","decisions":[],"invocation":{"args":{"after":"| Which TESTS are slow? (per test and per spec, load time included) | `TIMES=<file> bash tests/run.sh` (or with `SPEC=a_spec,b_spec`) — writes `ms<TAB>spec<TAB>name` per test (a spec's top-level work as its `(load)` row) and prints the 10 slowest specs and tests. Opt-in like `COVER` (the default output is what the fences read). MEASURED 2026-09-29: 79 s wall, 77 s in tests; 74% of tests < 10 ms; the top 20 tests = 44%, top 100 = 64%; load time negligible. ★ THE SUITE RUNS IN PARALLEL BY DEFAULT (`tests/prun.lua`): spec files across worker processes, each its own state home, balanced by the last run's measured per-spec time (cache `stdpath(cache)/cartograph/test-times.tsv`); workers derived = min(cores, total / slowest spec); `JOBS=N` exact, `JOBS=1` (or a `SPEC=` run without JOBS) the serial runner. One summary line on stderr, like the serial one; a worker with no summary FAILS the run by name. 77.6 s serial → 12.1 s, per-test rows identical; the floor is the slowest spec (treesitter_spec ~12 s). |\n| WHICH SPECS EXECUTE this line — a per-spec line-coverage map (which tests a mutation must run; which lines no test reaches) | `COVER=<file> COVER_SPEC=1 bash tests/run.sh` — rows `spec<TAB>lua/cartograph/<file>:<line>` for every line a spec executed, its LOAD-time fixture work included (the hook goes in before the specs load); parallel workers each write a part, concatenated. ⚠ JIT OFF under COVER (LuaJIT calls no hook from a compiled trace — a hot loop would read as unexecuted), so the run is ~10x slower (whole suite ~6 min) and a measurement of the JIT or of allocation skips itself by name. Without COVER_SPEC: the union, `<file>:<line>` (CART-0990). FIRST RUN 2026-10-05: 694,511 rows over 348 specs — and it surfaced CART-1479 (15 erlbif tests red only under that worker grouping). CART-1480 |","file":".claude/skills/cartograph/references/questions.md","before":"| Which TESTS are slow? (per test and per spec, load time included) | `TIMES=<file> bash tests/run.sh` (or with `SPEC=a_spec,b_spec`) — writes `ms<TAB>spec<TAB>name` per test (a spec's top-level work as its `(load)` row) and prints the 10 slowest specs and tests. Opt-in like `COVER` (the default output is what the fences read). MEASURED 2026-09-29: 79 s wall, 77 s in tests; 74% of tests < 10 ms; the top 20 tests = 44%, top 100 = 64%; load time negligible. ★ THE SUITE RUNS IN PARALLEL BY DEFAULT (`tests/prun.lua`): spec files across worker processes, each its own state home, balanced by the last run's measured per-spec time (cache `stdpath(cache)/cartograph/test-times.tsv`); workers derived = min(cores, total / slowest spec); `JOBS=N` exact, `JOBS=1` (or a `SPEC=` run without JOBS) the serial runner. One summary line on stderr, like the serial one; a worker with no summary FAILS the run by name. 77.6 s serial → 12.1 s, per-test rows identical; the floor is the slowest spec (treesitter_spec ~12 s). |"},"touched":[".claude/skills/cartograph/references/questions.md"],"verb":"edit"},"verb":"edit","bytes":817,"decided_by":"tactic"}],"hand":0,"replay":[{"id":"1791217330.843728-edit","invocation":{"args":{"after":"-- (the hook itself is installed BEFORE the specs load — see COVER_EARLY above — so a spec's top-level fixture work counts)","file":"tests/run.lua","before":"local cover\nif vim.env.COVER and vim.env.COVER ~= '' then\n    cover = {}\n    debug.sethook(function (_, line)\n        local i = debug.getinfo(2, 'S')\n        local s = i and i.short_src\n        if s and s:find('lua/cartograph/', 1, true) then\n            local t = cover[s]\n            if not t then t = {}; cover[s] = t end\n   